        <h3 class="text-lg font-medium mb-4">Notes</h3>
        <ol class="text-sm text-ink-light space-y-3 list-decimal pl-5">
          <li id="fn1">Hayek's description of the price system as a "marvel" appears in "The Use of Knowledge in Society" (1945), where he argues that prices communicate dispersed information without any participant needing to know the full picture. The mechanism is brilliant for coordination within given frameworks—but, as I argue here, cannot generate those frameworks itself.</li>
          <li id="fn2">The observation that markets presuppose rather than create their institutional foundations draws on economic sociology, particularly Polanyi's <em>The Great Transformation</em> (1944). Property rights, contract enforcement, and boundaries around legitimate exchange are political settlements, not market discoveries.</li>
          <li id="fn3">The distinction between epistemic questions (about facts) and evaluative questions (about what matters) is a foundational insight from moral philosophy. Measurement improvements resolve the former but often multiply the latter—what I call the asymmetry between epistemic and evaluative progress.</li>
          <li id="fn4">The concept of "constitutive" coordination—where mechanisms shape rather than merely aggregate preferences—builds on work in institutional economics and organizational theory. The feedback loop I describe (measurable → salient → legitimate → binding → optimized) is my synthesis of several related observations about how measurement creates reality rather than just recording it.</li>
        </ol>
